<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Test run settings for Goop.

  Select it in Visual Studio with:
      Test > Configure Run Settings > Select Solution Wide runsettings File

  From the command line, pass it with the "settings" option:
      dotnet test dotnet\Goop.sln  (settings) dotnet\goop.runsettings
  scripts\build.ps1 already does this.

  (Note: XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, which is why the CLI
  option above is written in parentheses rather than spelled literally.)

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: TargetPlatform.

  VSTest decides the test host's bitness on its own, and the default is not
  always x64. If it picks x86, every test that touches goop_native.dll dies with
  BadImageFormatException, because a 32-bit host cannot load a 64-bit native
  library. The managed projects already force PlatformTarget=x64, but that
  governs the ASSEMBLIES, not the host process VSTest launches to run them.
  Both have to agree, so it is pinned in both places.

  This is the same class of bug as the Prefer32Bit trap in
  Directory.Build.props, just one layer further out, and it is harder to spot
  because the build is completely clean and only the test run fails.
-->
<RunSettings>

  <RunConfiguration>
    <!-- The whole point of the file. -->
    <TargetPlatform>x64</TargetPlatform>

    <!-- Run serially. Slower, but a native crash (access violation, heap
         corruption) then takes down one host instead of racing several, and
         the surviving output tells you which framework it was. Worth it in an
         interop project. -->
    <MaxCpuCount>1</MaxCpuCount>

    <ResultsDirectory>.\TestResults</ResultsDirectory>
  </RunConfiguration>

  <!--
    NATIVE DEBUGGING OF TESTS is not configurable here. It is a Visual Studio
    setting, because VSTest owns the host process:

        Test > Options > check "Enable native code debugging"

    Turn it on, then "Debug Test" on an interop test steps into C++ exactly the
    way the Gallery profile does. Turn it back off afterwards; it slows down
    every test run, including pure-managed ones.

    If you would rather not fight the test host at all, reproduce the failing
    call in Goop.Gallery and debug that instead. Its launchSettings.json
    already has native debugging turned on.
  -->

</RunSettings>
